{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791733533,"nonce":"Z3pJkPFgLVGbNnsWi0MfuZbBwJ0=","title":"GC allocation statistics hide small allocations in the TLAB fast path","message":"ChimpMonk's allocation regression checks use hl.Gc.stats().totalAllocated. On the local Ash 0.1.5 release (SHA-256 161234e0b500c064541be3aeb756941b72a6c0b0bacf3f028283a5f4bdf26fcd), a program retains thirty-two 16-byte buffers and verifies their contents, yet the measured allocation is zero. The same bytecode with ASH_GC_TLAB=0 reports the expected 512 bytes. Default hybrid in both runs; no JIT override.\n\nReproducer is in the sibling ChimpMonk tree, repro/ash_gc_stats/StatsRepro.hx (self-contained, no engine dependency):\nhaxe -cp ../chimpmonk/repro/ash_gc_stats -main StatsRepro -hl /tmp/ash-gc-stats.hl\ntarget/release/ash /tmp/ash-gc-stats.hl\nASH_GC_TLAB=0 target/release/ash /tmp/ash-gc-stats.hl\n\nObserved JSON: {\"checksum\":496,\"observedBytes\":0,\"requestedBytes\":512} versus {\"checksum\":496,\"observedBytes\":512,\"requestedBytes\":512}. The checksum proves the retained buffers are used. The measurement subtracts statistics-reading overhead.\n\nThe current gc_stats implementation explicitly returns bytes_allocated, which charges an entire 32 KB region at TLAB refill, and always returns zero allocationCount. This is collector-pressure accounting, not sufficiently precise allocation accounting for small operations. Taking the least of short windows therefore returns zero for code that allocates on every call; longer windows can differ because of refill boundaries and recycled-span sizes. ChimpMonk validation originally allocated 336 unnecessary bytes per call for body diagnostic paths; default accounting hid those while exact diagnostic accounting exposed them. A corrected report-only validation batch and an identical empty-report control still measured 688128 versus 685952 bytes with TLABs enabled, making an exact comparison fail even though both create the same 80 bytes per report.\n\nPlease expose an accurate opt-in allocation-measurement path, preferably scoped to the calling worker/thread and including allocated bytes and object count. Keep the normal TLAB fast path and its pressure counters fast; avoid adding shared atomic work to every allocation merely for diagnostics. Reading statistics must not silently report no allocation for retained objects. Document which counters are logical allocation versus handed-out region capacity. Leave cross-worker accounting scope explicit.\n\nAcceptance: the reproducer reports 512 allocated bytes with the accurate counter while TLABs remain enabled; empty measurements report zero after read-overhead correction; batch cost agrees with repeated single-allocation cost; focused tests cover native/interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM allocation paths and workers; benchmark the diagnostic mechanism's overhead. Current workaround for measurements is ASH_GC_TLAB=0. ChimpMonk git-bug 4dc944769e3558ee337ec53e0f5c094c06381b843d259c6eaa71efddd62dadb0 tracks the caller-side correction. No Ash source changes made in this investigation.","files":null}]}